WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.970
Imagine living in a city

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.880
where you can afford beautiful homes.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.310
A city that combines
quality with affordability.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.330
Around the world, this
idea of a livable city

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.563
increasingly seems like a pipe dream.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.100
Housing unaffordability
is not just a crisis

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.460
for the United States.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.740
You see it in cities
as diverse as Caracas,

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.453
Moscow, Rio de Janeiro.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.940
The UN estimates that
despite the pandemic,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.480
over 60% of the world's population

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.543
will live in cities by 2030.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.760
Urbanization is going to keep growing

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:41.900
at a tremendous rate.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.920
And so we're really going to
need to look at how do we build

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.120
in a manner that's
livable, it's affordable.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.210
There is somewhere though

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.053
that seems to be keeping
the housing crisis at bay.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.510
The Austrian capital, Vienna,

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.920
is one of the most affordable
major cities in the world.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.900
It also tops the charts
for quality of life.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.330
And how they got there,
may offer up some tips

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.330
for cities globally.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.710
This is Aspern Seestadt,
in the suburbs of Vienna.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.350
One of the largest new
urban developments currently

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.240
under construction in Europe.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.560
Seestadt is being built
on a former airfield

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.933
and has a man-made swimming
lake at its center.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.690
60 to 70% of the apartments built here

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.250
will be allocated to affordable housing.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.663
Some of which are designed
by their occupants.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.300
We all know how tough it is

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.380
to bring new urban quarters to life,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.800
to make them feel like
the old parts of the city

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.800
that have this patina that we all love.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.743
So we introduce a new model
of collaborative housing.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.640
Self developed multi-family
housing originated

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.870
in Berlin and Freiburg,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.480
sort of emanating from
the squatters movements.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.650
These are groups of people
and oftentimes families

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:07.090
or people who are organized
around a certain ideology

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.910
like the environment or
living in solidarity.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.930
They will work to design

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.523
and build their own multi-family housing.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.370
Collaborative housing is
one of the innovative ways

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.250
subsidized apartments are
being built in Vienna.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.860
Now, apart from being
something really special

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.780
of having the opportunity
to design your own apartment

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.820
within a subsidized housing project,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.490
what is very important about this project

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.690
is that they think about so many things.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.290
They will introduce
regularly coworking spaces,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.450
rooms for seminars, or a big kitchen

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.200
that can be used by everybody.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.050
But the most important
part is that these people

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.310
that have worked together on
designing their own apartments

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.830
and their own building form a
community, a lively community.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.160
So we have found out that they
work as a community magnet

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.040
and they are the ones that will bring life

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.393
to a new urban quarter.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.440
When it comes to affordability,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.323
Seestadt is not an exception.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.310
Vienna is growing rapidly

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.080
and to meet the demand, every year,

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:24.600
it builds at least 7,000
affordable housing units.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.960
About half of all the
apartments built in the city.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.470
62% of the Viennese
population already live

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.123
in this type of accommodation.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.820
Public housing blocks,
which are apartments

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.120
that belong entirely to the city of Vienna

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.700
are just one part of the
affordable housing portfolio.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.920
The city has been building
them at a steady pace

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.493
since the 1920s.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.490
Many other countries also
tried to make this model work,

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.177
but few have succeeded.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.390
Public housing summons this vision

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.540
of cold monstrous towers.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.390
Many were, at the time
of their construction

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:11.470
following World War II,
very utopian projects.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.270
Their planners imagined

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.620
a kind of modernist egalitarian society

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.060
which might have succeeded
if they were funded

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.190
and maintained and
supported, but they were not.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:26.860
In the United States, the result

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:30.780
is that you have public
housing units that lack heating

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.190
and public housing units
that are just not safe

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.820
for the occupants.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.340
Compounded by the racial composition

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.020
of public housing residents,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.160
this has these deeply
troubling implications.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.690
You have both quality of life
issues and then general bias

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.490
and discriminatory attitudes
toward their residents.

00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:52.950
That mean that people
perceive public housing

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.583
as a threat, unfortunately.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:58.830
The stigma of public housing

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.290
has translated into U.S. policy.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.600
And a rule called the Faircloth Amendment

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.140
currently caps the number
of public housing units

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.463
at the level it was in 1999.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:11.310
Municipalities need to either sell

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.113
or demolish existing
buildings to build new ones.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.213
And this is all compounded
by restrictive zoning laws.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:24.160
When you have neighborhoods
or parts of entire cities

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.130
that are zoned for single family homes,

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:30.790
those neighborhoods are
effectively segregated.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.230
By controlling the nature of construction

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:34.640
through zoning codes,

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.640
you limit how much housing
can be made anywhere.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.670
That has had the result of
not a single U.S. county

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.680
having enough affordable housing

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.530
for its current population right now.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.490
In the '90s, Vienna also
took a radical decision

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:51.843
about its public housing,

00:05:52.677 --> 00:05:54.877
but the city went in
the opposite direction.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.610
Public housing was always
well-maintained and high quality,

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.580
but to maximize its investment,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.490
Vienna decided to instead focus

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.480
on a model offering subsidies
to private nonprofit companies

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.913
to build what they called social housing.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:16.920
The entire model of
subsidized housing in Vienna

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.960
is very robust and it is very profitable

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:23.100
for the corporations that engage in it.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.550
It works on the basis
of a threefold subsidy.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:31.330
Affordable housing stands
and falls with land prices.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:36.270
So the city of Vienna has
a very active land policy,

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.953
buying land or mobilizing

00:06:39.953 --> 00:06:42.894
historical land reserves,

00:06:42.894 --> 00:06:46.220
and then either selling it or leasing it

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.290
to not-for-profit corporations

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:52.060
at a very, very low affordable price.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.060
The companies get subsidized
loans for construction.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.500
And if the apartments
are still too expensive,

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.163
there are subsidies for renters.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.210
What is special about Vienna
is that social housing

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:05.980
is a middle-class subsidy.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:10.360
It is not thought to be only for people

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:14.150
that have a weak
socio-economic background.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.940
It is thought to be actually
for almost everybody.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.940
And as your income
increases, as you get older,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.930
you aren't pushed out
of your social housing.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:27.870
And what this does is it keeps
people in these communities.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.900
That's a very different way

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:32.643
of looking at keeping communities intact.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:39.460
In the '90s, the construction
process also changed.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.950
Each company that wants
to build social housing

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:45.580
needs to submit their design
in a developer competition.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:49.330
One of the most important mechanisms

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:53.443
of ensuring sustainability
and quality are competitions.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.070
The designs are judged based
on the architectural quality

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:01.970
of the development,

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:03.820
the economic function of the building,

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:06.230
so that's how affordably
they can build it,

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.840
the environmental
performance of the building,

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.080
as well as the social mix.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.350
And so this is just ensuring

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.110
that there's a diversity of unit types,

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.030
so it's not just singles or couples.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:18.493
You get families, you
have elderly residents.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.410
The competitive aspect of
the developer competitions

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.730
definitely pushes people
to be more innovative

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:25.670
on a number of fronts.

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:28.420
They're really well designed,
high quality buildings,

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:33.000
low energy buildings, just
wondrous amenities, saunas,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:38.000
kindergartens, clinics,
libraries, community spaces.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:39.880
We almost always have balconies.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.098
And in many cases there
are even swimming pools

00:08:43.098 --> 00:08:44.443
on the rooftops.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.320
Developer competitions are
also one of the main factors

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.550
driving down the costs of construction.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.300
The construction costs
in Vienna are about half

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:55.690
of what they are in Seattle.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.690
And so you're getting these incredible

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.090
high quality buildings
for really a fraction

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.130
of what we would pay here in the U.S.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.470
for what I would say are buildings

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.843
that are not as well done.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.240
Another key element to
making this model work

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.400
is using space wisely.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.040
It would be a waste of money

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.530
to embark into subsidized
housing and then do low density.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.300
Of course, you have to do high density

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.640
because this results in
many, many more apartments

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.740
to the benefit of many,
many more young families.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.810
But density will work well

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.890
only if you combine it with high quality.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:41.570
And I'm talking about high
architectural quality,

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.140
I'm talking about high level
public transport access,

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:48.543
and I'm talking about vast green spaces.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.870
So the way we go about density

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:56.740
is to create really vast accessible parks

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:01.370
and to concentrate construction
at the edges of these parks.

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:04.160
They don't have a significant
number of high rises,

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.110
but there is this broad density.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:09.530
The core is five to eight stories

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:11.980
for kilometers and kilometers
in every direction.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.060
Today, Vienna invests in a mix of public

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.530
and subsidized housing,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:20.560
but just building new
apartments isn't enough.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:23.530
The city is also committed
to actively maintaining

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:27.603
and retrofitting its stock of
over 500,000 affordable flats.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.890
There's this process in Vienna called

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.670
the gentle urban renewal.

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.590
And what this is is the city
will go into neighborhoods

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:39.390
where gentrification is
becoming more mainstream

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.670
and the effects of it are
becoming more visible.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.940
They will upgrade the social housing

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.050
to ensure that it
maintains a similar quality

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.480
to the new market rate housing.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.490
They will develop amenities

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.120
to ensure that schools
don't become overcrowded.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:55.190
They'll add a floor or two
to the roof of a building.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.860
This could become either
market rate or social housing.

00:10:57.860 --> 00:10:59.600
What this does is it allows more people

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:00.710
to live in the neighborhood.

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.760
It subsidizes the cost of
the energetic retrofit,

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.380
because there's also the
energetic component as well.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.280
And so they'll rehabilitate
the facade of a building.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:12.200
They'll add ventilation to ensure

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:13.980
that these buildings are comfortable,

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:15.930
there aren't mold issues, they're healthy.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.260
So the city is really
taking an active approach

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:22.290
in ensuring that these buildings
are comfortable, habitable

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:23.440
and also climate-proof.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.470
In the wake of the pandemic,

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.110
the affordability crisis
in cities worldwide

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:34.963
has become even more visible.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:37.150
Rent always eats first,

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:42.150
that means that medical
care, clothing, even food

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.810
are costs that renters sacrifice

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:47.870
in order to keep a home
over their family's head.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.200
During the pandemic, the kind of plight

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.750
of low-income tenants really expanded

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.820
because the number of
people who are out of work,

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:58.820
just ballooned and suddenly households

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.230
were confronting
precarity in their housing

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:03.963
for the first time.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:07.900
Vienna's model isn't a solution

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:10.680
that can easily be copy and pasted,

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.823
but all the cities are paying attention.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:18.300
It will take more than just
changes in policy though.

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.520
Attitudes about how and where we live

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:21.693
will need to shift too.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:25.250
In the U.S., there's a lot of desire

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.680
to live in a detached
house, own a single car.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.560
For families like mine
where we don't own a car,

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.800
we have two children,
we get around by bike,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.730
it's density and ample social housing

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:40.280
that really allows for
that kind of lifestyle.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:43.880
There is this generational
flip where families like ours

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.350
and especially those
that are younger than us

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:49.850
have no shot at affordable
housing or market rate housing

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.140
in a lot of cities.

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:53.510
And I think that we're going to see places

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.720
really start to look at adding a density

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:57.760
and hopefully in a smart way

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.680
that doesn't bring more
cars, more congestion

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.220
so it's more sustainable in the future.

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:06.767
Vienna is doing things that
everyone should be aiming for.

